solution.p:
The dominating term of f in the denominator is
\sqrt{x^2}=\abs{x} so divide the numerator and denominator of f by \sqrt{x^2},
then take limits.

  \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+1}}
  \amp = \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+1}}\cdot \frac{\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2}}}{\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2}}}
  \amp = \lim_{x\to\infty} \frac{\frac{x}{\abs{x}}}{\sqrt{\frac{x^2+1}{x^2}}}
  \amp = \lim_{x\to\infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+\frac{1}{x^2}}} \text{ for } x\gt 0
  \amp =\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+0}}
  \amp = 1 
.

