statement.p:
Let f be continuous on [a,b] and differentiable on (a,b). If the one-sided limits
\lim_{h\to0^+}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \amp \amp \amp \lim_{h\to0^-}\frac{f(b+h)-f(b)}{h}
exist, then we say f is differentiable on [a,b].

solution.p:
The one-sided limit of the difference quotient does not exist at x=0 for f;
therefore f is differentiable on
(0,\infty) and not differentiable on [0,\infty).

We state (without proof) that \gp(x) = 3\sqrt{x}/2.
Note that \lim_{x\to 0^+}\gp(x) = 0;
again, this limit is easier to evluate than the limit of the difference quotient.

