statement.p:
f(x) = \sqrt{x^5(1-x)},
domain is [0,1],
\fp(x) = \frac{(5-6x)x^{3/2}}{2\sqrt{1-x}}

solution.p:
\lim_{h\to0^+}\frac{f(0+h)-f(0)}h =0;
note also that \lim_{x\to0^+}\fp(x) = 0.
So f is differentiable at x=0.

\lim_{h\to0^-}\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}h =-\infty;
note also that \lim_{x\to1^-}\fp(x) = -\infty.
So f is not differentiable at x=1.

statement.p:
f(x) = \cos\left(\sqrt{x}\right),
domain is [0,\infty),
\fp(x) = -\frac{\sin\left(\sqrt{x}\right)}{2\sqrt{x}}

