\end{tikzpicture}

solution.p:
A pattern might jump out at you;
note how the we end up multiplying by the old power and the new power is reduced by 1.
We also always multiply by (-1).

li.p:
Recognize that y = \ln\mathopen{}\left(4x^3-2x^2\right)\mathclose{} is the composition of
f(x) = \ln(x) and g(x) = 4x^3-2x^2.
Also, recall that

  \lzoo{x}{\ln(x)} = \frac{1}{x}
.
This leads us to:

  \yp \amp = \frac{1}{4x^3-2x^2} \cdot \lzoo{x}{4x^3-2x^2}
  \amp = \frac{1}{4x^3-2x^2} \cdot \left(12x^2-4x\right)
  \amp  = \frac{12x^2-4x}{4x^3-2x^2}
  \amp = \frac{4x(3x-1)}{2x(2x^2-x)}
  \amp = \frac{2(3x-1)}{2x^2-x}
.
Note that \ln\mathopen{}\left(4x^3-2x^2\right)\mathclose{}=\ln\mathopen{}\left(4x^2(x-1/2)\right)\mathclose{} was only defined for x \gt 1/2,
so the result of \yp=\frac{2(3x-1)}{2x^2-x} is only valid for x \gt 1/2 as well.

