solution.p:
As suggested above,
we start by taking the natural log of both sides then applying implicit differentiation.

  y \amp = x^x\amp
  \ln(y) \amp = \ln(x^x)\amp\amp\text{(apply logarithm rule)}
  \ln(y) \amp = x\ln(x) \amp\amp\text{(now use implicit differentiation)}
  \lzoo{x}{\ln(y)} \amp = \lzoo{x}{x\ln(x)}
  \frac{\yp}{y} \amp = \ln(x) + x\cdot\frac{1}{x}\amp
  \frac{\yp}{y} \amp = \ln(x) + 1\amp
  \yp \amp = y\left(\ln(x) +1\right) \amp\amp\text{(substitute }y=x^x\text{)}
  \yp \amp = x^x\left(\ln(x) +1\right)\amp
.

