section.p:
Now solve for x_1:

  x_1=x_0-\frac{f(x_0)}{\fp(x_0)}
.

Since we repeat the same geometric process to find x_2 from x_1, we have

  x_2=x_1-\frac{f(x_1)}{\fp(x_1)}
.

li.p:
Create successive approximations iteratively;
given an approximation x_n,
compute the next approximation x_{n+1} as

  x_{n+1} = x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{\fp(x_n)}
.

solution.p:
To begin, we compute \fp(x)=3x^2-2x.
Then we apply the Newton's Method algorithm,
outlined in .





      \begin{aligned}
        x_1\amp=1-\frac{f(1)}{\fp(1)}\\
        \amp=1-\frac{1^3-1^2-1}{3\cdot 1^2-2\cdot 1}\\
        \amp =2\end{aligned}







      \begin{aligned}
        x_2\amp =2-\frac{f(2)}{\fp(2)}\\
        \amp =2-\frac{2^3-2^2-1}{3\cdot 2^2-2\cdot 2}\\
        \amp=1.625\end{aligned}







      \begin{aligned}
        x_3\amp =1.625-\frac{f(1.625)}{\fp(1.625)}\\
        \amp = 1.625-\frac{1.625^3-1.625^2-1}{3\cdot 1.625^2-2\cdot 1.625}\\
        \amp \approx 1.48579\end{aligned}







      \begin{aligned}
        x_4 \amp =1.48579-\frac{f(1.48579)}{\fp(1.48579)}\\
        \amp \approx  1.46596\end{aligned}







      \begin{aligned}
        x_5 \amp = 1.46596 - \frac{f(1.46596)}{\fp(1.46596)}\\
        \amp \approx 1.46557\end{aligned}

