solution.p:
By choosing x as we did
(instead of letting x be the distance along the land),
we make the expression under the square root simple.
We now create the cost function.

  \text{Cost} \amp ={}\amp\amp \text{land cost}              \amp\amp +\text{water cost}
  \amp\amp\amp   \$50\times \text{land distance} \amp\amp +\$130 \times \text{water distance}
  \amp\amp\amp   50(5000-x)                      \amp\amp +130\sqrt{x^2+1000^2}
.

There is no maximum sum;
the fundamental equation has only 1 critical value that
corresponds to a minimum.

