solution.p:
Recall that by integrating a speed function we get distance traveled.
We have information about v(t);
we will use Simpson's Rule to approximate \ds \int_a^b v(t)\, dt.

webwork.pg-code:
$a = -1;
$b = 1;
$n = 4;
$f = Formula("x^2");
$F = Formula("x^3/3");
$delta_x = ($b - $a)/$n;
@l = ();
$trap = 0;
$simp = 0;
foreach $i (0..$n-1) {
  push @l, $i*$delta_x + $a;
}
$flip = 1;    
foreach $i (@l) {
  $trap += $f->eval(x=>$i) + $f->eval(x=>($i + $delta_x));
  if ($flip == 1) {
    $simp += $f->eval(x=>$i) + 4*$f->eval(x=>($i + $delta_x)) + $f->eval(x=>($i+2*$delta_x));
    $flip = 0;
  } else {
    $flip = 1;
  }
}
$trap = $trap*$delta_x/2;
$simp = $simp*$delta_x/3;
$exact = $F->eval(x=>$b) - $F->eval(x=>$a);

