section.p:
In we defined the definite integral as the signed area under the curve. In that section we had not yet learned the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, so we only evaluated special definite integrals which described nice, geometric shapes. For instance, we were able to evaluate
\int_{-3}^3\sqrt{9-x^2}\, dx = \frac{9\pi}{2}
as we recognized that f(x) = \sqrt{9-x^2} described the upper half of a circle with radius 3.

