li.p:
The denominators in each term are perfect squares;
we are adding \ds \sum_{n=4}^\infty \frac{1}{n^2}
(note  we start with n=4, not n=1).
This series will converge.
Using the formula from ,
we have the following:

  \infser \frac1{n^2} \amp = \sum_{n=1}^3 \frac1{n^2} +\sum_{n=4}^\infty \frac1{n^2}
  \infser \frac1{n^2} - \sum_{n=1}^3 \frac1{n^2} \amp =\sum_{n=4}^\infty \frac1{n^2}
  \frac{\pi^2}{6} - \left(\frac11+\frac14+\frac19\right) \amp = \sum_{n=4}^\infty \frac1{n^2}
  \frac{\pi^2}{6} - \frac{49}{36} \amp = \sum_{n=4}^\infty \frac1{n^2}
  0.2838\amp \approx \sum_{n=4}^\infty \frac1{n^2}

p.m:
S_n = \left\{\begin{array}{cc} -\frac{n+1}{2} \amp n \text{ is odd } \\
\frac{n}{2} \amp n \text{ is even }
\end{array} \right.

li.p:
a_n = \frac1{n(n+3)};
using partial fractions,
the resulting telescoping sum reduces to S_n = \frac13\left(1+\frac12+\frac13-\frac1{n+1}-\frac1{n+2}-\frac1{n+3}\right)

introduction.p:
Break the Harmonic Series into the sum of the odd and even terms:

  \infser \frac1n = \infser \frac{1}{2n-1}+\infser \frac{1}{2n}
.

