solution.p:
This is the antiderivative of e^{-x^2};
while we can write it out as a series,
we cannot write it out in terms of elementary functions.
We can evaluate the definite integral
\ds \int_0^1e^{-x^2}\, dx using this antiderivative;
substituting 1 and 0 for x and subtracting gives

  \int_0^1e^{-x^2}\, dx = 1-\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{5\cdot 2!}-\frac{1}{7\cdot3!} + \frac{1}{9\cdot4!}\cdots
.

f^{(n)}(x) = (-1)^ne^{-x};
at x=0, f^{(n)}(0)=-1 when n is odd and
f^{(n)}(0)=1 when n is even.

