li.p:
Both \vec v and 2\vec v are sketched in .
Make note that 2\vec v does not start at the terminal point of \vec v;
rather, its initial point is also the origin.

The figure suggests that 2\vec v is twice as long as \vec v.
We compute their magnitudes to confirm this.

  \norm{\vec v} \amp = \sqrt{2^2+1^2}
  \amp = \sqrt{5}.
  \norm{2\vec v}\amp =\sqrt{4^2+2^2}
  \amp = \sqrt{20}
  \amp = \sqrt{4\cdot 5} = 2\sqrt{5}
.
As we suspected, 2\vec v is twice as long as \vec v.

section.p:
Property of holds the key. If we divide \vec v by its magnitude, it becomes a vector of length 1. Consider:
\snorm{ rac{1}{
orm{\vec v}}\vec v} \amp =  rac{1}{
orm{\vec v}}
orm{\vec v} \amp 	ext{ (we can pull out \(\ds  rac{1}{
orm{\vec v}}\) as it is a positive scalar)} \amp = 1 .

