subsection.p:
Using the key property of the dot product, we have

abla f\cdot \vec u = 
orm{
abla f}\,\vnorm u \cos(	heta) = 
orm{
abla f}\cos(	heta) , where 	heta is the angle between the gradient and \vec u. (Since \vec u is a unit vector, \vnorm{u} = 1.) This equation allows us to answer the three questions stated previously.

solution.p:
shows the surface plotted from two different perspectives. In each, the gradient is drawn at P with a dashed line (because of the nature of this surface, the gradient points into the surface). Let \vec u = \la u_1, u_2a be the unit vector in the direction of 
abla f at P. Each graph of the figure also contains the vector \la u_1, u_2, 
orm{
abla f\,}a. This vector has a run of 1 (because in the xy-plane it moves 1 unit) and a rise of 
orm{
abla f}, hence we can think of it as a vector with slope of 
orm{
abla f} in the direction of 
abla f, helping us visualize how steep the surface is in its steepest direction.

