solution.p:
We follow ,
to find M, M_x and M_y:

  M \amp = \iint_R (x+y+2)\, dA = \int_0^1\int_0^1 (x+y+2)\, dx\, dy =3\,\text{g}
  M_x \amp = \iint_R y(x+y+2)\, dA = \int_0^1\int_0^1 y(x+y+2)\, dx\, dy =\frac{19}{12}
  M_y \amp = \iint_R x(x+y+2)\, dA = \int_0^1\int_0^1 x(x+y+2)\, dx\, dy =\frac{19}{12}
.

Thus the center of mass is (\overline{x},\overline{y}) = \left(0,\frac{364}{33\pi}\right) \approx (0,3.51).
The center of mass is indicated in ;
note how it lies outside of R!

statement.p:
R is the rectangle with corners (1,-3), (1,2),
(7,2) and (7,-3);
\delta(x,y) = 5\,\text{g/cm}^2

R is the rectangle with corners (1,-3), (1,2),
(7,2) and (7,-3);
\delta(x,y) = (x+y^2)\,\text{g/cm}^2

R is the triangle with corners (-1,0),
(1,0), and (0,1);
\delta(x,y) = 2\,\text{lb/in}^2

R is the triangle with corners (0,0),
(1,0), and (0,1);
\delta(x,y) = (x^2+y^2+1)\,\text{lb/in}^2

R is the circle sector bounded by
x^2+y^2=25 in the first quadrant;
